\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within one benchmark.}
tinyBenchmarks~\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks}, metabench~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} and anchor points~\citep{vivek2024anchor} choose \emph{examples} of a benchmark so that its full score can be recovered from a few. We choose \emph{whole benchmarks} and predict \emph{other} benchmarks, so the target is not a function of what was run. Their latent-ability estimators are the nearest machinery for predicting from a sparse observed set, and the model of Section~\ref{sec:method} is of the same kind.

\paragraph{Low-dimensional structure of model scores.}
Observational scaling laws~\citep{ruan2024observational} find that the scores of about a hundred language models lie close to a low-dimensional capability space, and Sloth~\citep{polo2024sloth} uses such skills to predict scores across model families. Both fit the structure and use it to extrapolate along compute or size; neither asks which $k$ benchmarks to run for a new model or how large $k$ must be. Agreement between benchmarks also depends on the analysis choices~\citep{perlitz2024benchbench}, which is why our protocol fixes the matrix definition and the margins before any result is computed (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}).

\paragraph{Cold-start elicitation, matrix completion and factor models.}
Which items to ask a new user to rate is the cold-start problem of recommenders~\citep{rashid2002getting,golbandi2011adaptive,elahi2016survey}; a new model is a new user, a benchmark an item and $k$ the number of ratings. Active learning for cold-start matrix factorisation~\citep{houlsby2014coldstart} and probabilistic PCA with missing data~\citep{tipping1999ppca} supply our inference model; matrix-completion theory~\citep{candes2009exact,candes2010power,mazumder2010spectral} assumes the observed entries are sampled at random, whereas here the new row's observed set is \emph{chosen}. Benchmark matrices are small and nearly dense, scores are continuous with benchmark-specific noise, and the evaluator wants a calibrated $k$ rather than a ranking of items.

\paragraph{Optimal design and adaptive testing.}
Choosing a subset of columns to predict the rest is column subset selection~\citep{boutsidis2009css}; greedy selection carries guarantees through the submodularity ratio~\citep{das2011submodular,bian2017guarantees} and, for Bayesian A-optimality, from Bayesian experimental design~\citep{chaloner1995bayesian}. Multidimensional computerized adaptive testing selects items by A- or D-optimal criteria to measure a latent ability vector~\citep{mulder2009multidimensional}, which is the same design problem with benchmarks as items. We claim no new selection method. What we add is the answer for a concrete setting---real leaderboard matrices with missing cells, whole models held out---together with a sufficiency rule for $k$ and the conditions under which it holds.
